# DESCRIPTION
# This problem walks through a step-by-step Euclid algorithm to find the GCF of two positive integers.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Number theory)
## DBchapter(Divisibility)
## DBsection(Division algorithm)
## Date(September 5, 2016)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## MO(1)

# update 2022.10.30

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'niceTables.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

@primes = (2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19);

$gcf =
    $primes[ random(0, 2) ] * $primes[ random(0, 2) ] * $primes[ random(0, 2) ];

$p1 = $primes[ random(3, 5) ];
do { $p2 = $primes[ random(3, 6) ] } until ($p1 != $p2);

@nums = ($p1 < $p2) ? ($gcf * $p2, $gcf * $p1) : ($gcf * $p1, $gcf * $p2);

do {
    $n   = scalar @nums;
    $rem = $nums[ $n - 2 ] % $nums[ $n - 1 ];
    push @nums, $rem;
} until ($rem == 0);

@table = ([
    PGML('[`GCF([$nums[0]],[$nums[1]])`]'),
    PGML('[`=`]'),
    PGML('[`GCF(`][___]{$nums[1]}[`,`][___]{$nums[2]}[`)`]')
]);
for ($i = 2; $i < $#nums; $i++) {
    push @table,
        [
            '', PGML('[`=`]'),
            PGML('[`GCF(`][___]{$nums[$i]}[`,`][___]{$nums[$i+1]}[`)`]')
        ];
}

BEGIN_PGML

Use Euclid's Algorithm to build up the following table.

[@ DataTable(\@table) @]***

The result shows that [`GCF([$nums[0]],[$nums[1]])=`][___]{$nums[$#nums-1]}

END_PGML

@ans_table = ([
    PGML('[`GCF([$nums[0]],[$nums[1]])`]'), PGML('[`=`]'),
    PGML('[`GCF([$nums[1]],[$nums[2]])`]')
]);
for ($i = 2; $i < $#nums; $i++) {
    push @ans_table,
        [ '', PGML('[`=`]'), PGML('[`GCF([$nums[$i]],[$nums[$i+1]])`]') ];
}

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Recall that if [`r`] is the remainder of [`n \div k`], then
[``` GCF(n,k) = GCF(k,r) ```]
so the table is created by successive applications of this until the remainder is 0.  The resulting table is

[@ DataTable(\@ans_table) @]***

and then [`GCF([$nums[0]],[$nums[1]]) = [$nums[$#nums-1]] `]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
